Object.assign(window.HM,{"runs": {"S-k01": {"q": "What is the battery capacity of the Steam Deck OLED?", "prompt": "What is the battery capacity of the Steam Deck OLED?", "named": false, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\">The Steam Deck OLED has a <strong>50 Wh</strong> battery. This is <strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark></strong>: Valve's tech specs page lists it as \"50Whr battery. 3-12 hours of gameplay.\" (<a href=\"https://www.steamdeck.com/en/tech\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">steamdeck.com/en/tech</a>), as of 2026-10-10.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"1\">The LCD model's 40 Wh figure is <strong><mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark></strong>. It comes from news coverage of the OLED launch, such as <a href=\"https://www.thesixthaxis.com/2023/11/10/steam-deck-oled-announced-with-longer-battery-life-price-drop-for-original-models/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Sixth Axis</a>. I didn't confirm it on Valve's site.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://www.steamdeck.com/en/tech\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Steam Deck tech specs (Valve)</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://www.steamdeck.com/oled\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Steam Deck OLED (Valve)</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://www.thesixthaxis.com/2023/11/10/steam-deck-oled-announced-with-longer-battery-life-price-drop-for-original-models/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Sixth Axis</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "Steam Deck OLED tech specs battery capacity site:steamdeck.com"}, {"t": "open", "host": "steamdeck.com", "path": "/en/tech", "url": "https://www.steamdeck.com/en/tech", "s": "read", "said": "The Steam Deck OLED has a 50 Wh battery."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.steamdeck.com/en/tech", "host": "steamdeck.com", "title": "steamdeck.com/en/tech", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.thesixthaxis.com/2023/11/10/steam-deck-oled-announced-with-longer-battery-life-price-drop-for-original-models/", "host": "thesixthaxis.com", "title": "The Sixth Axis", "s": "found"}, {"u": "https://www.steamdeck.com/oled", "host": "steamdeck.com", "title": "Steam Deck OLED (Valve)", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-s3": {"q": "How long do I have to return an item to IKEA?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. How long do I have to return an item to IKEA?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\">At IKEA US, you have 365 days to return an unopened item and 180 days to return an opened one. Both windows need proof of purchase and earn a full refund. <strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">Official</mark></strong>, read on <a href=\"https://www.ikea.com/us/en/customer-service/returns-claims/return-policy/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IKEA US: How to Return or Exchange Products</a>, checked 2026-10-10.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Exceptions (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> IKEA doesn't accept returns at Planning Studios or Pick Up Points.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Other exclusions (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>):</strong> The search summary listed plants, cut fabric, custom countertops, as-is items, and modified or damaged items as non-returnable. The page I opened didn't list these. They come from IKEA's separate FAQ pages, which I didn't open.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Mattresses (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>):</strong> The same summary said mattresses get a 90-day exchange under \"Love it or exchange it.\" I didn't open that page.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Other countries:</strong> These windows are for the US and may differ elsewhere.</li></ul><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://www.ikea.com/us/en/customer-service/returns-claims/return-policy/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IKEA US: How to Return or Exchange Products</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://www.ikea.com/us/en/customer-service/returns-claims/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IKEA US: Return policies</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "IKEA US return policy days to return"}, {"t": "open", "host": "ikea.com", "path": "/us/en/customer-service/returns-claims/return-policy/", "url": "https://www.ikea.com/us/en/customer-service/returns-claims/return-policy/", "s": "read", "said": "Unopened items: 365 days."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.ikea.com/us/en/customer-service/returns-claims/return-policy/", "host": "ikea.com", "title": "IKEA US: How to Return or Exchange Products", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.ikea.com/us/en/customer-service/returns-claims/", "host": "ikea.com", "title": "IKEA US: Return policies", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-k03": {"q": "How long does copyright last in the US for a book written today?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. How long does copyright last in the US for a book written today?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">For a book written today, US copyright lasts for the author's life plus 70 years. That is <strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark></strong>, from the U.S. Copyright Office FAQ on duration and 17 U.S.C. § 302(a).</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">Some cases differ, also <strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark></strong>:</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Joint authors:</strong> the term runs for 70 years after the last surviving author dies (17 U.S.C. § 302(b)).</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Anonymous, pseudonymous, or work-made-for-hire books:</strong> the term is 95 years from first publication or 120 years from creation, whichever ends first (Copyright Office FAQ; § 302(c)).</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"unchecked\" data-s=\"\">Copyright terms run to the end of the calendar year in which they would expire (§ 305). I did not open that section, so treat this point as <strong><mark class=\"lb lb-unchecked\">unchecked</mark></strong>.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">I read the statute text on Cornell's Legal Information Institute (law.cornell.edu), not on a government site. This is current as of 2026-10-10.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://www.copyright.gov/help/faq/faq-duration.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">U.S. Copyright Office, FAQ: How long does copyright last?</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/17/302\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">17 U.S.C. § 302 (Cornell LII)</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "open", "host": "copyright.gov", "path": "/circs/circ15a.pdf", "url": "https://www.copyright.gov/circs/circ15a.pdf", "s": "unread", "said": "The excerpt you provided doesn't contain readable text that answers this."}, {"t": "open", "host": "law.cornell.edu", "path": "/uscode/text/17/302", "url": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/17/302", "s": "read", "said": "§ 302(a): For works created on or after January 1, 1978, copyright lasts for the author's life plus 70 years."}, {"t": "open", "host": "copyright.gov", "path": "/help/faq/faq-duration.html", "url": "https://www.copyright.gov/help/faq/faq-duration.html", "s": "read", "said": "- Individual author: The page says copyright generally lasts \"the life of the author plus an additional 70 years.\"\n- Anonymous, pseudonymous, or work made for hire: The term is 95 years after publication or 120 years after creation, \"whichever expires first.\"\n- Joint works: The page doesn't address joint works."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.copyright.gov/help/faq/faq-duration.html", "host": "copyright.gov", "title": "U.S. Copyright Office, FAQ: How long does copyright last?", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/17/302", "host": "law.cornell.edu", "title": "17 U.S.C. § 302 (Cornell LII)", "s": "read"}], "labels": ["official", "unchecked"], "n": {"search": 0, "open": 3, "read": 2, "blocked": 1}}, "N-k04b": {"q": "Which IRS form gives me more time to file my federal tax return, and how much time does it give?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. Which IRS form gives me more time to file my federal tax return, and how much time does it give?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Form 4868</strong> gives you more time to file. It is the \"Application for Automatic Extension of Time To File U.S. Individual Income Tax Return.\"</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>How much time (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> It gives an automatic extension of about six months. That moves the filing deadline from the April due date to October 15.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>How to ask (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> File Form 4868 by the April due date. You can also request the extension by paying online and checking the extension box, or through IRS Free File.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>It doesn't extend payment (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> The extension covers filing only. Any tax you owe is still due by the April date. Interest and penalties can apply to unpaid tax after that.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Living abroad (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> U.S. citizens and resident aliens who live outside the U.S. on the regular due date may get an automatic two-month extension instead. That is a separate rule and doesn't require Form 4868.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Today is 2026-10-10, so the October 15 extended deadline for the 2025 tax year is five days away. I did not check the exact 2026 calendar date. October 15, 2026 falls on a Thursday, so no weekend shift applies.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\">Source: IRS, <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/extension-of-time-to-file-your-tax-return\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Extension of time to file your tax return</a>, read October 10, 2026.</p>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "open", "host": "irs.gov", "path": "/forms-pubs/extension-of-time-to-file-your-tax-return", "url": "https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/extension-of-time-to-file-your-tax-return", "s": "read", "said": "- Form: Form 4868, \"Application for Automatic Extension of Time To File U.S."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/extension-of-time-to-file-your-tax-return", "host": "irs.gov", "title": "Extension of time to file your tax return", "s": "read"}], "labels": ["official"], "n": {"search": 0, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-k05b": {"q": "How long should I wash my hands?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. How long should I wash my hands?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">Scrub for at least 20 seconds. <strong>(<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>, CDC, US guidance)</strong></p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">The CDC's steps are: wet your hands, apply soap, lather the backs of your hands, between your fingers and under your nails, scrub for at least 20 seconds, rinse under clean running water, then dry with a clean towel or air dryer. <strong>(<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>)</strong></p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">If soap and water aren't available, use a hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol. <strong>(<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>)</strong> The CDC page gives no rub time for sanitizer.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/clean-hands/about/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CDC, About Handwashing</a> (read today, 2026-10-10)</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/clean-hands/about/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CDC, About Handwashing</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "open", "host": "cdc.gov", "path": "/clean-hands/about/index.html", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/clean-hands/about/index.html", "s": "read", "said": "Scrub your hands with soap for at least 20 seconds."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.cdc.gov/clean-hands/about/index.html", "host": "cdc.gov", "title": "CDC, About Handwashing", "s": "read"}], "labels": ["official"], "n": {"search": 0, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-k06": {"q": "Does GitHub's free plan include private repositories?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. Does GitHub's free plan include private repositories?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Yes. GitHub Free includes unlimited private repositories (as of 2026-10-10).</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\"><strong>Private repos: <mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>.</strong> The GitHub Free plan for personal accounts includes unlimited private repositories with unlimited collaborators. It is listed as a \"limited feature set.\" (<a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/githubs-plans\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub Docs: plans</a>)</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"1\"><strong>Pricing page: <mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>.</strong> The Free plan lists \"Unlimited public/private repositories.\" (<a href=\"https://github.com/pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub pricing</a>)</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Limits on private repos: <mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>.</strong> These features are not on Free:<ul><li>Protected branches, code owners, and required reviewers need GitHub Pro.</li><li>Pages and deployment protection rules are public-repo only on Free.</li><li>Actions gets 2,000 minutes per month, Packages gets 500 MB, and Codespaces gets 120 core hours per month.</li><li>Support is community-only.</li></ul></li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The pricing page fetch returned a summary instead of the full comparison table, so I didn't cross-check the Team and Enterprise per-user prices. I left them out.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/githubs-plans\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub Docs: GitHub's plans</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub pricing</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "open", "host": "docs.github.com", "path": "/en/get-started/learning-about-github/githubs-plans", "url": "https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/githubs-plans", "s": "read", "said": "Yes."}, {"t": "open", "host": "github.com", "path": "/pricing", "url": "https://github.com/pricing", "s": "read", "said": "The Free plan includes \"Unlimited public/private repositories.\" The comparison table also lists private-repository collaborators as unlimited on Free."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/githubs-plans", "host": "docs.github.com", "title": "GitHub Docs: plans", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://github.com/pricing", "host": "github.com", "title": "GitHub pricing", "s": "read"}], "labels": ["official"], "n": {"search": 0, "open": 2, "read": 2, "blocked": 0}}, "N-k07": {"q": "What is the default timeout in the Python requests library?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. What is the default timeout in the Python requests library?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">There is no default timeout. Requests waits indefinitely unless you set one. <strong>(<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>)</strong></p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The Requests documentation says: \"By default, requests do not time out unless a timeout value is set explicitly. Without a timeout, your code may hang for minutes or more.\"</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Single value:</strong> <code>timeout=5</code> applies to both the connect and read timeouts.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Separate values:</strong> <code>timeout=(3.05, 27)</code> sets connect and read separately.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Waiting forever:</strong> <code>timeout=None</code> is the same as the default.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Caveat:</strong> The timeouts are not wall-clock limits, so a request can take longer in real time than the value you set. The read timeout is the wait between bytes from the server, not a cap on the whole response.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Always pass <code>timeout=</code> on calls to external servers.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\">Source: Requests docs, Advanced Usage, \"Timeouts\" section (latest docs, read 2026-10-10): <a href=\"https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/advanced/#timeouts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/advanced/#timeouts</a></p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/advanced/#timeouts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Requests: Advanced Usage, Timeouts</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "open", "host": "requests.readthedocs.io", "path": "/en/latest/user/advanced/", "url": "https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/advanced/#timeouts", "s": "read", "said": "Advanced Usage ¶\n\nThis document covers some of Requests more advanced features."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/advanced/#timeouts", "host": "requests.readthedocs.io", "title": "Requests: Advanced Usage, Timeouts", "s": "read"}], "labels": ["official"], "n": {"search": 0, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-k08": {"q": "What was the US population in the 2020 census?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. What was the US population in the 2020 census?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\">The 2020 Census counted <strong>331,449,281</strong> U.S. residents as of April 1, 2020. This figure is <strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark></strong>, from the Census Bureau's <a href=\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2021/2020-census-apportionment-results.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2020 Census apportionment press release</a>. It covers the 50 states and the District of Columbia.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"1\">The apportionment count is a different number: <strong>331,108,434</strong>. It covers the 50 states only, plus overseas military and federal civilian employees and their dependents. It excludes D.C. and Puerto Rico. This figure is <strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark></strong>, but I did not see it on the press release page. It comes from the Census Bureau's search results, which cite its <a href=\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment-2020-table01.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">apportionment table</a>. I did not open that table.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">The resident count was up 7.4% from 308,745,538 in 2010 (<strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark></strong>, same Census Bureau search results; I did not open the underlying table).</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://www.census.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2021/2020-census-apportionment-results.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2020 Census Apportionment Results Delivered to the President</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment-2020-table01.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Census Bureau apportionment table 1 (PDF)</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "2020 Census apportionment population count United States census.gov"}, {"t": "open", "host": "census.gov", "path": "/newsroom/press-releases/2021/2020-census-apportionment-results.html", "url": "https://www.census.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2021/2020-census-apportionment-results.html", "s": "read", "said": "The 2020 Census resident population of the United States was 331,449,281 as of April 1, 2020."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.census.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2021/2020-census-apportionment-results.html", "host": "census.gov", "title": "2020 Census apportionment press release", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment-2020-table01.pdf", "host": "www2.census.gov", "title": "apportionment table", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["official"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-k09": {"q": "Did a study find that you need 10,000 steps a day to live longer?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. Did a study find that you need 10,000 steps a day to live longer?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">No. The studies don't say you need 10,000 steps a day to live longer. They find most of the lower-mortality benefit comes at lower step counts. It is an observational association, not proof that steps cause longer life.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"0\"><strong>Women, average age 72 (JAMA Internal Medicine, 2019):</strong> Women averaging about 4,400 steps a day had a lower death rate than those averaging about 2,700. The benefit leveled off around 7,500 steps. The authors write that the 10,000-step goal has limited scientific basis. This is <mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>: I read search summaries of the paper, not the paper itself. Source: <a href=\"https://whish.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Step-Volume-Intensity-Mortality_JAMA-IntMedicine-2019-.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stanford copy of the paper</a>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"1\"><strong>15 cohorts pooled (Lancet Public Health, 2022):</strong> The risk of death fell as steps rose, then leveled off at about 6,000–8,000 steps a day for adults 60 and older. For adults under 60 it leveled off at about 8,000–10,000. Compared with the least active quarter (median 3,553 steps), the most active quarter (median 10,901 steps) had a hazard ratio of 0.47, meaning about half the death rate. Note that group's median is about 10,900, and the paper does not call 10,000 a threshold. This is <mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>: <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9289978\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PMC full text</a>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><strong>57 studies from 35 cohorts (Lancet Public Health, 2025):</strong> The authors suggest 7,000 steps a day as a meaningful target for the general population. They say 10,000 can still be a viable target for people who are more active. Compared with 2,000 steps a day, 7,000 steps went with about a 47% lower risk of death (hazard ratio 0.53). The 7,000-step target and the 47% figure come from search summaries and the journal's highlight text. The Lancet page returned a 403 error, so I did not read the full findings. The paper is <a href=\"https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpub/article/PIIS2468-2667(25)00164-1/fulltext\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ding et al.</a>.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">As of October 2026, the roughly 7,000-step mark is the most recent figure, and the older studies point to a similar range.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://whish.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Step-Volume-Intensity-Mortality_JAMA-IntMedicine-2019-.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stanford copy of Lee et al. 2019</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9289978\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paluch et al. 2022, PMC</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpub/article/PIIS2468-2667(25)00164-1/fulltext\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ding et al. 2025, The Lancet Public Health</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "Lancet Public Health 2025 daily steps 7000 meta-analysis Ding all-cause mortality"}, {"t": "search", "q": "Lee 2019 JAMA Internal Medicine step volume intensity mortality older women 4400 steps"}, {"t": "open", "host": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "path": "/35247352/", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35247352/", "s": "unread", "said": "The page you provided doesn't contain any study findings."}, {"t": "open", "host": "thelancet.com", "path": "/journals/lanpub/article/PIIS2468-2667(25)00164-1/fulltext", "url": "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpub/article/PIIS2468-2667(25)00164-1/fulltext", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "open", "host": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "path": "/articles/PMC9289978", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9289978", "s": "read", "said": "The Findings section reports that mortality risk falls as daily steps rise, leveling off at about 6,000–8,000 steps per day for adults aged 60 and older and at about 8,000–10,000 steps per day for adults under 60."}, {"t": "search", "q": "Ding Lancet Public Health 2025 \"Daily steps and health outcomes in adults\" systematic review dose-response abstract"}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://whish.stanford.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Step-Volume-Intensity-Mortality_JAMA-IntMedicine-2019-.pdf", "host": "whish.stanford.edu", "title": "Stanford copy of the paper", "s": "found"}, {"u": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9289978", "host": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "title": "PMC full text", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpub/article/PIIS2468-2667(25)00164-1/fulltext", "host": "thelancet.com", "title": "Ding et al.", "s": "blocked"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 3, "open": 3, "read": 1, "blocked": 2}}, "S-l-secondhand": {"q": "Did Bill Gates say 640K ought to be enough for anybody?", "prompt": "Did Bill Gates say 640K ought to be enough for anybody?", "named": false, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Almost certainly not. Gates has denied it, and nobody has found a contemporary source showing he said it.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\"><strong>His denial (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>, via Quote Investigator quoting the original column):</strong> In his syndicated newspaper column in the <em>Daily News of Los Angeles</em>, 22 January 1996, a reader asked him about it. He answered, \"I've said some stupid things and some wrong things, but not that.\" He added that no one involved in computers would say any amount of memory is enough for all time. I read this on Quote Investigator, not in the column itself.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\"><strong>No 1981 source (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>, Quote Investigator):</strong> The usual 1981 date comes from a 1995 <em>Washington Post</em> list. The only tie to 1981 is that the IBM PC launched that year.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Earliest trace (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>, Quote Investigator):</strong> An <em>InfoWorld</em> editorial from 29 April 1985 said, \"When we set the upper limit of PC-DOS at 640K, we thought nobody would ever need that much memory.\" It was not an interview and named no source. It also didn't attribute the words to Gates. Computerworld says an <em>InfoWorld</em> piece in 1988 gave a version of the line to Gates without quotation marks or a reference.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Other denial and expert view (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>, Computerworld):</strong> Gates repeated the denial in a 2001 <em>U.S. News &amp; World Report</em> interview. Fred Shapiro, editor of the <em>Yale Book of Quotations</em>, still considers the quote apocryphal.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">I didn't find the 1996 column or the 2001 interview text itself. Gates has said the 640K limit became a real constraint, but that's different from saying it was enough.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://quoteinvestigator.com/2011/09/08/640k-enough/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Quote Investigator: 640K Ought to be Enough for Anyone</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://www.computerworld.com/article/1564172/the-quotable-bill-gates-in-his-own-words.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Computerworld: He never really said that about 640KB of memory being enough</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "Bill Gates \"640K\" quote denied never said Quote Investigator"}, {"t": "search", "q": "gatesnotes OR microsoft.com Bill Gates 640K \"never said\" memory quote"}, {"t": "open", "host": "quoteinvestigator.com", "path": "/2011/09/08/640k-enough/", "url": "https://quoteinvestigator.com/2011/09/08/640k-enough/", "s": "read", "said": "Denial (1996): Gates answered a reader question in his syndicated newspaper column, published in the Daily News of Los Angeles on January 22, 1996 (Business section, p."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://quoteinvestigator.com/2011/09/08/640k-enough/", "host": "quoteinvestigator.com", "title": "Quote Investigator: 640K Ought to be Enough for Anyone", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.computerworld.com/article/1564172/the-quotable-bill-gates-in-his-own-words.html", "host": "computerworld.com", "title": "Computerworld: He never really said that about 640KB of memory being enough", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["secondhand"], "n": {"search": 2, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-k11": {"q": "How long is overtime in an NBA game?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. How long is overtime in an NBA game?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\">An NBA overtime period is <strong>5 minutes</strong> (<strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark></strong>). Rule No. 5, Section II says: \"All overtime periods of play will be five minutes.\" That is the NBA's own rulebook page: <a href=\"https://official.nba.com/rule-no-5-scoring-and-timing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rule No. 5: Scoring and Timing</a>.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"1\">If the score is still tied after an overtime period, the game goes to another 5-minute period, and this repeats until one team leads. The search result's summary of the 2025-26 rules said the same (<strong><mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark></strong>, from a search snippet of the <a href=\"https://cdn.nba.com/manage/2026/01/Official-2025-26-NBA-Playing-Rules.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2025-26 NBA playing rules PDF</a>; I did not open the PDF).</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"2\">The 2026 All-Star Game is an exception. Per the <a href=\"https://cdn.nba.com/manage/2026/02/2026-NBA-All-Star-Game-Rules.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2026 All-Star Game rules</a>, overtime there is played to a target score, not a clock (<strong><mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark></strong>, from a search snippet; I did not open the PDF).</li></ul><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://official.nba.com/rule-no-5-scoring-and-timing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rule No. 5: Scoring and Timing, official.nba.com</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://cdn.nba.com/manage/2026/01/Official-2025-26-NBA-Playing-Rules.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official 2025-26 NBA Playing Rules (PDF)</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://cdn.nba.com/manage/2026/02/2026-NBA-All-Star-Game-Rules.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2026 NBA All-Star Game Rules (PDF)</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "NBA official rulebook overtime period five minutes"}, {"t": "open", "host": "official.nba.com", "path": "/rule-no-5-scoring-and-timing/", "url": "https://official.nba.com/rule-no-5-scoring-and-timing/", "s": "read", "said": "Overtime periods are five minutes long."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://official.nba.com/rule-no-5-scoring-and-timing/", "host": "official.nba.com", "title": "Rule No. 5: Scoring and Timing", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://cdn.nba.com/manage/2026/01/Official-2025-26-NBA-Playing-Rules.pdf", "host": "cdn.nba.com", "title": "2025-26 NBA playing rules PDF", "s": "found"}, {"u": "https://cdn.nba.com/manage/2026/02/2026-NBA-All-Star-Game-Rules.pdf", "host": "cdn.nba.com", "title": "2026 All-Star Game rules", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-k12b": {"q": "When is the opening ceremony of the 2028 Summer Olympics?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. When is the opening ceremony of the 2028 Summer Olympics?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The opening ceremony of the 2028 Summer Olympics is on <strong>July 14, 2028</strong>, a Friday, in Los Angeles.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0,1\"><strong>Date (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> LA28, the organizing committee, gives July 14, 2028 on two of its pages. <a href=\"https://la28.org/en/newsroom/LA28_Announces_Games_Dates.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Games dates announcement</a> and <a href=\"https://la28.org/en/newsroom/la28-unveils-2028-olympic-paralympic-opening-and-closing-ceremony-locations.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ceremony locations announcement</a>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Venue (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> The ceremony is shared between the LA Memorial Coliseum and the Stadium in Inglewood. The same LA28 page says more details on the ceremonies \"will be identified and shared at a later date.\"</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"unchecked\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Start time (<mark class=\"lb lb-unchecked\">unchecked</mark>):</strong> The LA28 pages I opened give no start time. A search summary said 5:00 p.m. PT / 8:00 p.m. ET, but I couldn't confirm that on an official page, so don't rely on it yet.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">This is current as of 2026-10-10.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://la28.org/en/newsroom/LA28_Announces_Games_Dates.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LA28 Announces Official Games Dates</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://la28.org/en/newsroom/la28-unveils-2028-olympic-paralympic-opening-and-closing-ceremony-locations.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LA28 Unveils 2028 Olympic and Paralympic Opening and Closing Ceremony Locations</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "LA28 Olympic Games opening ceremony date July 14 2028 la28.org"}, {"t": "open", "host": "la28.org", "path": "/en/newsroom/LA28_Announces_Games_Dates.html", "url": "https://la28.org/en/newsroom/LA28_Announces_Games_Dates.html", "s": "read", "said": "The Olympic Opening Ceremony is set for July 14, 2028."}, {"t": "open", "host": "la28.org", "path": "/en/newsroom/la28-unveils-2028-olympic-paralympic-opening-and-closing-ceremony-locations.html", "url": "https://la28.org/en/newsroom/la28-unveils-2028-olympic-paralympic-opening-and-closing-ceremony-locations.html", "s": "read", "said": "Date: July 14, 2028\nVenue: Shared between the Los Angeles Memorial Coliseum and the Stadium in Inglewood\nStart time: The page doesn't state one."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://la28.org/en/newsroom/LA28_Announces_Games_Dates.html", "host": "la28.org", "title": "Games dates announcement", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://la28.org/en/newsroom/la28-unveils-2028-olympic-paralympic-opening-and-closing-ceremony-locations.html", "host": "la28.org", "title": "ceremony locations announcement", "s": "read"}], "labels": ["official", "unchecked"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 2, "read": 2, "blocked": 0}}, "N-s4": {"q": "How much RAM does the iPhone 15 have?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. How much RAM does the iPhone 15 have?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\">The iPhone 15 has 6 GB of RAM. That figure is <mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>, because Apple doesn't publish RAM for the phone.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\"><strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">Official</mark>:</strong> I opened the <a href=\"https://support.apple.com/en-us/111831\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">iPhone 15 Tech Specs page</a> and it lists storage (128, 256 and 512 GB) but no RAM figure.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"1\"><strong><mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">Secondhand</mark>:</strong> The 6 GB figure comes from two places.<ul><li>MacRumors reported that Xcode 15 release-candidate files list 6 GB for the iPhone 15 and 15 Plus (and 8 GB for the Pro models). I didn't open the MacRumors article. That detail came through a search summary.</li><li>The <a href=\"https://techinsights.com/ja/node/54018\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechInsights teardown</a> of the iPhone 15 (A2846) identifies the memory as a 6 GB LPDDR5X package. The A3092 teardown reports the same memory from a different supplier.</li></ul></li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">This applies to the iPhone 15 and 15 Plus, as of 2026-10-10. The Pro models differ at 8 GB, per the same MacRumors report.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://support.apple.com/en-us/111831\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">iPhone 15 Tech Specs, Apple Support</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://techinsights.com/ja/node/54018\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechInsights iPhone 15 A2846 teardown</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://techinsights.com/ja/node/54440\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechInsights iPhone 15 A3092 teardown</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "iPhone 15 tech specs apple.com support RAM"}, {"t": "open", "host": "support.apple.com", "path": "/en-us/111831", "url": "https://support.apple.com/en-us/111831", "s": "read", "said": "No, the page doesn't state the iPhone 15's RAM or memory."}, {"t": "search", "q": "iPhone 15 6GB RAM A16 Bionic confirmed Xcode teardown"}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://support.apple.com/en-us/111831", "host": "support.apple.com", "title": "iPhone 15 Tech Specs page", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://techinsights.com/ja/node/54018", "host": "techinsights.com", "title": "TechInsights teardown", "s": "found"}, {"u": "https://techinsights.com/ja/node/54440", "host": "techinsights.com", "title": "TechInsights iPhone 15 A3092 teardown", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 2, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "S-hero": {"q": "Did the WHO say processed meat is as dangerous as smoking?", "prompt": "Did the WHO say processed meat is as dangerous as smoking?", "named": false, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">No. The WHO's cancer agency said the opposite. It put processed meat in the same classification as smoking, but it said that doesn't make them equally dangerous.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\"><strong>What the classification is (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>).</strong> The WHO page says processed meat is \"Group 1, carcinogenic to humans.\" Group 1 means \"there is convincing evidence that the substance causes cancer.\" The page also says the categories show how strong the evidence is. They don't measure \"the likelihood that cancer will occur.\" The page is dated 23 September 2026. Source: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/cancer-carcinogenicity-of-the-consumption-of-red-meat-and-processed-meat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WHO Q&amp;A on red and processed meat</a>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"1,2\"><strong>Where the comparison came from (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>).</strong> When processed meat was classified in 2015, the group that ranks cancer evidence, the International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC), listed it alongside tobacco and asbestos. That is the source of the \"as bad as smoking\" headlines. A search summary of IARC's own Q&amp;A said being in the same category \"does not mean that they are all equally dangerous.\" I could not open the PDFs (the <a href=\"https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/11/Monographs-QA_Vol114.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IARC Q&amp;A</a> and <a href=\"https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/pr240_E.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">press release</a>) to confirm the wording. The WHO page also didn't contain it.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\"><strong>How big the risk is (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>).</strong> The same summary said each 50 g of processed meat eaten daily raises colorectal cancer risk by about 18%. That is a relative increase, and I didn't verify it against the original. The summary also said the individual risk remains small, though it matters for public health because so many people eat processed meat. For comparison, smoking raises lung cancer risk many times over. That comparison is from memory and I did not check it.</li></ul><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/cancer-carcinogenicity-of-the-consumption-of-red-meat-and-processed-meat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WHO: Carcinogenicity of red and processed meat</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/11/Monographs-QA_Vol114.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IARC Q&amp;A, Volume 114</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/pr240_E.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IARC press release</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "IARC Monographs processed meat Group 1 carcinogenic \"does not mean\" same level of risk as smoking"}, {"t": "open", "host": "who.int", "path": "/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/cancer-carcinogenicity-of-the-consumption-of-red-meat-and-processed-meat", "url": "https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/cancer-carcinogenicity-of-the-consumption-of-red-meat-and-processed-meat", "s": "read", "said": "The page doesn't mention smoking, asbestos, or an 18% per 50g figure, and it doesn't say processed meat is equally dangerous to them."}, {"t": "open", "host": "iarc.who.int", "path": "/wp-content/uploads/2018/11/Monographs-QA_Vol114.pdf", "url": "https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/11/Monographs-QA_Vol114.pdf", "s": "unread", "said": "I can't find that information in the content you provided."}, {"t": "open", "host": "iarc.who.int", "path": "/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/pr240_E.pdf", "url": "https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/pr240_E.pdf", "s": "unread", "said": "I can't find that claim in the content you provided."}, {"t": "open", "host": "awareness.who.int", "path": "/features/qa/cancer-red-meat/en/", "url": "http://awareness.who.int/features/qa/cancer-red-meat/en/", "s": "blocked", "said": "getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND awareness.who.int"}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/cancer-carcinogenicity-of-the-consumption-of-red-meat-and-processed-meat", "host": "who.int", "title": "WHO Q&amp;A on red and processed meat", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/11/Monographs-QA_Vol114.pdf", "host": "iarc.who.int", "title": "IARC Q&amp;A", "s": "unread"}, {"u": "https://www.iarc.who.int/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/pr240_E.pdf", "host": "iarc.who.int", "title": "press release", "s": "unread"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 4, "read": 1, "blocked": 3}}, "N-s6": {"q": "How much will it cost to renew US passports for two adults and two children?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. How much will it cost to renew US passports for two adults and two children?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Renewing passport books for two adults and two children costs <strong>$530</strong> in government fees. The two children can't renew, though. They have to apply for new passports in person.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Fees are per person and come from the State Department's fee page, last updated March 19, 2026.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Applicant</th><th>Application fee</th><th>Facility fee</th><th>Each</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adult renewal, book (form DS-82)</td><td>$130</td><td>none</td><td>$130</td></tr><tr><td>Child under 16, book (form DS-11)</td><td>$100</td><td>$35</td><td>$135</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"calculated\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Total for books (<mark class=\"lb lb-calculated\">calculated</mark>):</strong> 2 × $130 + 2 × $135 = $260 + $270 = <strong>$530</strong>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"calculated\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Cards instead of books (<mark class=\"lb lb-calculated\">calculated</mark>):</strong> adults $30 each and children $15 + $35 each, so 2 × $30 + 2 × $50 = <strong>$160</strong>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"calculated\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Book and card together (<mark class=\"lb lb-calculated\">calculated</mark>):</strong> adults $160 each and children $115 + $35 each, so 2 × $160 + 2 × $150 = <strong>$620</strong>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Extras (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> expedited service is $60 per application. Return shipping in 1-3 days is $26.87 per application, and it isn't offered for card-only applications. Mailing the application to the State Department by Priority Mail Express costs extra and varies by location.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Adults can only renew with the DS-82 if they meet the eligibility rules. Otherwise they apply as first-timers, with the $35 facility fee added.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Caveats:</strong></p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The main travel.state.gov pages returned errors when I tried to open them. I read the same fee page on two other State Department sites, adoptions.state.gov and childabduction.state.gov. Both showed the March 2026 figures.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">A State Department fee chart titled \"October 2026\" also exists, but I couldn't read its contents. Check travel.state.gov/passportfees before you pay in case any amounts changed.</li></ul><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">Sources (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://adoptions.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Passport Fees, State Department mirror</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://childabduction.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Passport Fees, second State Department mirror</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://travel.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Passport Fees, travel.state.gov</a> (listed in search results; it returned an error when I opened it)</li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "open", "host": "travel.state.gov", "path": "/content/travel/en/passports/need-passport/fees.html", "url": "https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/need-passport/fees.html", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "open", "host": "travel.state.gov", "path": "/content/travel/en/passports/have-passport/renew.html", "url": "https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/have-passport/renew.html", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "search", "q": "travel.state.gov passport fees renew adult child under 16"}, {"t": "open", "host": "travel.state.gov", "path": "/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html", "url": "https://travel.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "open", "host": "travel.state.gov", "path": "/content/dam/passports/forms-fees/Accessible_Passport_Fee_Chart_Apr_2026.pdf", "url": "https://travel.state.gov/content/dam/passports/forms-fees/Accessible_Passport_Fee_Chart_Apr_2026.pdf", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "open", "host": "travel.state.gov", "path": "/en/passports/apply/child/under-16.html", "url": "https://travel.state.gov/en/passports/apply/child/under-16.html", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "search", "q": "passport fees $130 renewal $100 child $35 acceptance fee 2026"}, {"t": "open", "host": "adoptions.state.gov", "path": "/content/dam/tsg_aem/passports/documents/forms-fees/Passport_Fees_OCT_2026.pdf", "url": "https://adoptions.state.gov/content/dam/tsg_aem/passports/documents/forms-fees/Passport_Fees_OCT_2026.pdf", "s": "unread", "said": "I can't find the fee amounts in the content you provided."}, {"t": "open", "host": "adoption.state.gov", "path": "/content/dam/tsg_aem/passports/documents/forms-fees/Passport_Fee_Chart_English_Accessible_July_2026.pdf", "url": "https://adoption.state.gov/content/dam/tsg_aem/passports/documents/forms-fees/Passport_Fee_Chart_English_Accessible_July_2026.pdf", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 404 Not Found."}, {"t": "open", "host": "adoptions.state.gov", "path": "/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html", "url": "https://adoptions.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html", "s": "read", "said": "Passport fees (per the page)\n\nAdult renewal (eligible, by mail or online):\n- Passport book: $130\n- Passport card: $30\n- Book and card: $160\n\nChild under 16 (DS-11, applied in person):\n- Passport book: $100\n- Passport card: $15\n- Book and card: $115\n\nFacility acceptance fee: $35 per application, paid to the acceptance facility."}, {"t": "open", "host": "childabduction.state.gov", "path": "/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html", "url": "https://childabduction.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html", "s": "read", "said": "Adult renewal (16+, eligible, by mail or online)\n- Passport book: $130\n- Passport card: $30\n- Book and card: $160\n\nChild under 16 (in-person application, DS-11)\n- Passport book: $100 application fee + $35 facility acceptance fee\n- Passport card: $15 + $35\n- Book and card: $115 + $35\n\nAcceptance fee: $35 per application, paid to the acceptance facility for in-person applications (adult"}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://adoptions.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html", "host": "adoptions.state.gov", "title": "Passport Fees, State Department mirror", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://childabduction.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html", "host": "childabduction.state.gov", "title": "Passport Fees, second State Department mirror", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://travel.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/fees.html", "host": "travel.state.gov", "title": "Passport Fees, travel.state.gov", "s": "blocked"}], "labels": ["official", "calculated"], "n": {"search": 2, "open": 9, "read": 2, "blocked": 7}}, "S-l-unchecked": {"q": "What is the legal minimum tread depth for car tyres in the UK?", "prompt": "What is the legal minimum tread depth for car tyres in the UK?", "named": false, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"unchecked\" data-s=\"\">The legal minimum tread depth for car tyres in the UK is <strong>1.6mm</strong>. It applies across the central three-quarters of the tread, around the full circumference of the tyre. (<mark class=\"lb lb-unchecked\">unchecked</mark>)</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">I couldn't open any web pages in this session, so I'm giving this from memory and haven't read the official source.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Applies to:</strong> cars, and also light trailers. Other vehicle types have different rules. This is for Great Britain, and I believe Northern Ireland uses the same figure.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Penalty:</strong> up to £2,500 per illegal tyre and 3 penalty points per tyre. Both figures are from memory, and the maximum penalty is the one I'm least sure of.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Advice, not law:</strong> The RAC and tyre makers recommend replacing tyres at 3mm, because braking distances get noticeably longer below that.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">To check it yourself, look at GOV.UK's \"Check your tyres are safe\" page (gov.uk/check-tyres-are-safe). The underlying law is the Road Vehicles (Construction and Use) Regulations 1986, regulation 27, on legislation.gov.uk.</p>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}], "srcs": [], "labels": ["unchecked"], "n": {"search": 0, "open": 0, "read": 0, "blocked": 0}}, "N-l-official2": {"q": "How tall is the Statue of Liberty?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. How tall is the Statue of Liberty?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">The Statue of Liberty is 305 feet 1 inch (92.99 m) tall from the ground to the tip of the torch. <strong>(<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>)</strong></p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Statue alone:</strong> The NPS lists the \"top of base to torch\" height as 151 feet 1 inch (46.05 m). <strong>(<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>)</strong> One NPS fact sheet gives this as 46.5 m, so the NPS pages differ slightly in metric.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Pedestal and foundation:</strong> The NPS lists \"ground to pedestal\" as 154 feet 0 inches (46.94 m). <strong>(<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>)</strong></li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Heel to top of head:</strong> 111 feet 1 inch (33.86 m). <strong>(<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>)</strong></li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"calculated\" data-s=\"\">Check: 151'1\" + 154'0\" = 305'1\", which matches the listed total. <strong>(<mark class=\"lb lb-calculated\">calculated</mark>)</strong></p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/stli/learn/historyculture/statue-statistics.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NPS, Statue Statistics, Statue of Liberty National Monument</a>. The page didn't say \"as of\" a date, but these dimensions are fixed.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/stli/learn/historyculture/statue-statistics.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Statue Statistics, NPS</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "Statue of Liberty height National Park Service statistics"}, {"t": "open", "host": "nps.gov", "path": "/stli/learn/historyculture/statue-statistics.htm", "url": "https://www.nps.gov/stli/learn/historyculture/statue-statistics.htm", "s": "read", "said": "The page doesn't list a \"heel to torch\" measurement or a foundation height."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.nps.gov/stli/learn/historyculture/statue-statistics.htm", "host": "nps.gov", "title": "NPS, Statue Statistics, Statue of Liberty National Monument", "s": "read"}], "labels": ["official", "calculated"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-effort-low": {"q": "Did NASA say the Great Wall of China is visible from space?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. Did NASA say the Great Wall of China is visible from space?", "named": true, "effort": "low", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">No. NASA says the Great Wall is generally not visible to the naked eye from space.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\"><strong>From the Moon:</strong> the wall isn't visible. NASA calls the idea a myth. (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>: <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA, \"Great Wall\"</a>)</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>From low Earth orbit:</strong> it is hard to spot and may be impossible without a powerful telephoto lens, like the one used for Expedition 10 Commander Leroy Chiao's 2004 photo. (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>: same NASA page)</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"1\"><strong>Naked eye, per China's first astronaut:</strong> NASA's astronaut photography FAQ says Yang Liwei could not see it. The FAQ also says some recent high-resolution astronaut photos appear to show it. I took both points from the search summary of the <a href=\"https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FAQ</a> and did not open that page myself. (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>, search summary)</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"2\"><strong>Radar:</strong> the wall is easy to detect with radar, as in the 1994 Shuttle SIR-C/X-SAR images. I also took this from the search summary of NASA's pages and did not open them. (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>, search summary; see <a href=\"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/images/pia01838-space-radar-image-of-great-wall-of-china/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">JPL</a>)</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The \"only man-made object visible from space\" claim is a myth. As of 2026-10-10.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA: Great Wall</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA: Gateway to Astronaut Photography FAQ</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/images/pia01838-space-radar-image-of-great-wall-of-china/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA/JPL: Space Radar Image of Great Wall of China</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "NASA Great Wall of China visible from space"}, {"t": "open", "host": "nasa.gov", "path": "/image-article/great-wall/", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/", "s": "read", "said": "NASA says the Great Wall of China is not visible from the Moon."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/", "host": "nasa.gov", "title": "NASA, \"Great Wall\"", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/", "host": "eol.jsc.nasa.gov", "title": "FAQ", "s": "found"}, {"u": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/images/pia01838-space-radar-image-of-great-wall-of-china/", "host": "jpl.nasa.gov", "title": "JPL", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["official"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "N-effort-medium": {"q": "Did NASA say the Great Wall of China is visible from space?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. Did NASA say the Great Wall of China is visible from space?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">No, not in the usual sense. NASA's own pages say the Great Wall is not visible to the naked eye from space.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\"><strong>From the Moon (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> NASA says that \"despite myths to the contrary, the wall isn't visible from the moon, and is difficult or impossible to see from Earth orbit without the high-powered lenses used for this photo.\" The photo is Expedition 10 commander Leroy Chiao's from the International Space Station. Source: <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA, Great Wall</a>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"1\"><strong>From low Earth orbit (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> NASA's astronaut photography FAQ says \"The Great Wall is not visible to the naked eye from orbit, as recently demonstrated by China's first astronaut.\" It adds that the Wall does appear in some recent high-resolution astronaut photographs. Source: <a href=\"https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA Gateway to Astronaut Photography FAQ</a>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"2\"><strong>Radar (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>):</strong> This comes from a search summary of NASA's older \"China's Wall Less Great in View from Space\" feature, which I did not open. It says the wall shows up easily in radar images because of its steep, smooth sides. The pages I opened don't cover radar. Source: <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/vision/space/workinginspace/great_wall.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA feature</a>.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">So NASA debunks the claim that you can see the wall unaided. A telephoto lens or radar can pick it out. The pages were read on 2026-10-10 and could change.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA, Great Wall</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA Gateway to Astronaut Photography FAQ</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/vision/space/workinginspace/great_wall.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA, China's Wall Less Great in View from Space</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "NASA Great Wall of China visible from space"}, {"t": "open", "host": "nasa.gov", "path": "/image-article/great-wall/", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/", "s": "read", "said": "NASA says the Great Wall is not visible from the Moon, and it is hard or impossible to see from low Earth orbit without special equipment."}, {"t": "open", "host": "eol.jsc.nasa.gov", "path": "/faq/", "url": "https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/", "s": "read", "said": "The FAQ says: \"The Great Wall is not visible to the naked eye from orbit, as recently demonstrated by China's first astronaut\"\n\nIt also notes: \"The Wall does appear to be visible in some recent high-resolution astronaut photographs.\""}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/", "host": "nasa.gov", "title": "NASA, Great Wall", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/", "host": "eol.jsc.nasa.gov", "title": "NASA Gateway to Astronaut Photography FAQ", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.nasa.gov/vision/space/workinginspace/great_wall.html", "host": "nasa.gov", "title": "NASA feature", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 2, "read": 2, "blocked": 0}}, "N-effort-high": {"q": "Did NASA say the Great Wall of China is visible from space?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. Did NASA say the Great Wall of China is visible from space?", "named": true, "effort": "high", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">No. NASA says the opposite: the Great Wall is generally not visible to the naked eye from space.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0\"><strong>From orbit, naked eye (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> NASA's astronaut photography FAQ says \"The Great Wall is not visible to the naked eye from orbit.\" (<a href=\"https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA Gateway to Astronaut Photography FAQ</a>)</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"1\"><strong>From the Moon (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> The same FAQ says \"It is not visible from the Moon.\" NASA's 2009 image caption agrees: \"Despite myths to the contrary, the wall isn't visible from the moon.\" (<a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA, \"Great Wall\"</a>)</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\"><strong>With a camera (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>):</strong> The caption says the wall is \"difficult or impossible to see from Earth orbit without the high-powered lenses\" used for that photo. The photo was taken by ISS Expedition 10 commander Leroy Chiao. The FAQ adds that the wall \"does appear to be visible in some recent high-resolution astronaut photographs.\"</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Radar (<mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>):</strong> A search summary said radar detects the wall more easily, and that China's first astronaut, Yang Liwei, said he couldn't see it. I didn't open the underlying NASA pages (the 1994 Shuttle radar image and the \"China's Wall Less Great in View from Space\" article) to confirm either point.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The myth is that you can see the wall from space unaided. NASA's statements are about what you can see by eye versus with magnification. The NASA pages I read don't name the origin of the myth. They also don't address the related claim that it's the only human-made object visible from space.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">As of 2026-10-10.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA Gateway to Astronaut Photography FAQ</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA, \"Great Wall\"</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "NASA Great Wall of China visible from space"}, {"t": "open", "host": "nasa.gov", "path": "/image-article/great-wall/", "url": "https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/", "s": "read", "said": "NASA says the Great Wall is not visible from the Moon and is hard to see from Earth orbit without special equipment."}, {"t": "open", "host": "eol.jsc.nasa.gov", "path": "/faq/", "url": "https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/", "s": "read", "said": "The FAQ says the Great Wall is not visible to the naked eye from orbit:\n\n- \"The Great Wall is not visible to the naked eye from orbit\"\n\nIt also says the Wall is not visible from the Moon:\n\n- \"It is not visible from the Moon.\"\n\nOn photographs, it states:\n\n- \"The Wall does appear to be visible in some recent high-resolution astronaut photographs\"\n\nThe FAQ does not specifically mention the ISS, and i"}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://eol.jsc.nasa.gov/faq/", "host": "eol.jsc.nasa.gov", "title": "NASA Gateway to Astronaut Photography FAQ", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/", "host": "nasa.gov", "title": "NASA, \"Great Wall\"", "s": "read"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 2, "read": 2, "blocked": 0}}, "N-nosource3": {"q": "How old is the universe?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. How old is the universe?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The universe is about <strong>13.8 billion years old</strong>.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0,1\"><strong>13.8 billion years: <mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>.</strong> NASA's <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Universe overview</a> says \"Around 13.8 billion years ago, the universe expanded faster than the speed of light for a fraction of a second.\" That date is the start of cosmic inflation, so it dates the beginning of the universe. NASA's Hubble pages also use 13.8 billion years, as does its <a href=\"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/planck-mission-brings-universe-into-sharp-focus/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Planck release</a>.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"2\"><strong>13.787 ± 0.020 billion years: <mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>.</strong> This is the Planck 2018 value, which comes from the Planck team's analysis of the cosmic microwave background. I only found it on a Manifold Markets page citing the paper (<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">arXiv:1807.06209</a>). The paper's PDF wouldn't load as text, and the abstract doesn't state an age. Treat the exact digits as unconfirmed, though they round to NASA's 13.8.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">No one owns this fact, so the best evidence is the measurements NASA and ESA report, mainly the Planck mission's map of the cosmic microwave background. That is a model-based estimate, not an exact count. As of October 2026, I didn't check for newer results.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA Science: Universe overview</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/planck-mission-brings-universe-into-sharp-focus/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA JPL: Planck Mission Brings Universe Into Sharp Focus</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06209\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Planck 2018 results VI (arXiv)</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"3\"><a href=\"https://manifold.markets/ThothHermes/in-50-years-what-will-most-cosmolog\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Manifold Markets, citing Planck 2018</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "NASA age of the universe 13.8 billion years Planck WMAP"}, {"t": "search", "q": "Planck 2018 results VI cosmological parameters age of the universe arXiv"}, {"t": "open", "host": "arxiv.org", "path": "/pdf/1807.06209v3", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.06209v3", "s": "unread", "said": "The content you provided doesn't include the value."}, {"t": "open", "host": "arxiv.org", "path": "/abs/1807.06209", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06209", "s": "read", "said": "No, the abstract does not state the age of the universe."}, {"t": "open", "host": "aanda.org", "path": "/articles/aa/full_html/2020/09/aa33910-18/aa33910-18.html", "url": "https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2020/09/aa33910-18/aa33910-18.html", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "open", "host": "arxiv.org", "path": "/pdf/1807.06209v1", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.06209v1", "s": "unread", "said": "I couldn't find the Age [Gyr] value in the content you provided."}, {"t": "search", "q": "NASA Science \"age of the universe\" 13.8 billion years"}, {"t": "open", "host": "science.nasa.gov", "path": "/universe/overview/", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/", "s": "read", "said": "The page dates the universe's beginning to about 13.8 billion years ago."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/", "host": "science.nasa.gov", "title": "Universe overview", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/planck-mission-brings-universe-into-sharp-focus/", "host": "jpl.nasa.gov", "title": "Planck release", "s": "found"}, {"u": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06209", "host": "arxiv.org", "title": "arXiv:1807.06209", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://manifold.markets/ThothHermes/in-50-years-what-will-most-cosmolog", "host": "manifold.markets", "title": "Manifold Markets, citing Planck 2018", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 3, "open": 5, "read": 2, "blocked": 3}}, "N-neverpub": {"q": "What is the battery capacity of the iPhone 15 in mAh?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. What is the battery capacity of the iPhone 15 in mAh?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\">The iPhone 15 battery is about 3,349 mAh, but that figure is <mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark>. Apple doesn't publish a capacity.</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"0,1\"><strong><mark class=\"lb lb-official\">Official</mark> (Apple):</strong> Apple's iPhone 15 tech specs page (<a href=\"https://support.apple.com/en-us/111831\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">support.apple.com/en-us/111831</a>) lists only \"Built-in rechargeable lithium-ion battery\" and runtime estimates such as up to 20 hours of video playback. The main <a href=\"https://www.apple.com/iphone-15/specs/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">specs page</a> covers only current models and has no iPhone 15 capacity. I found no mAh figure on any Apple page.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"2,3\"><strong><mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">Secondhand</mark>:</strong> A Chinese regulatory database listing, as reported by MySmartPrice and repeated by <a href=\"https://hothardware.com/news/battery-capacities-apple-iphone-15-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HotHardware</a> and <a href=\"https://www.mactech.com/2023/09/15/battery-capacity-of-the-iphone-15-line-up-revealed-in-chinese-regulatory-database/amp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MacTech</a>, gives 3,349 mAh (12.981 Wh). I did not open the regulatory listing itself, so these outlets are my only source for the number. A search summary said some outlets swapped the Pro and Plus figures, but the iPhone 15 figure was consistent.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Not the answer:</strong> Earlier pre-launch leaks said about 3,877 mAh. The regulatory figure replaced them.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">This is as of 2026-10-10. Battery capacity is a design rating, so a real phone's usable capacity drops as the battery ages.</p><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://support.apple.com/en-us/111831\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Apple iPhone 15 tech specs</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://www.apple.com/iphone-15/specs/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Apple iPhone specs</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://hothardware.com/news/battery-capacities-apple-iphone-15-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HotHardware</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"3\"><a href=\"https://www.mactech.com/2023/09/15/battery-capacity-of-the-iphone-15-line-up-revealed-in-chinese-regulatory-database/amp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MacTech</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "iPhone 15 battery capacity mAh site:apple.com"}, {"t": "open", "host": "apple.com", "path": "/iphone-15/specs/", "url": "https://www.apple.com/iphone-15/specs/", "s": "read", "said": "The page doesn't include the iPhone 15's battery capacity."}, {"t": "open", "host": "support.apple.com", "path": "/en-us/111831", "url": "https://support.apple.com/en-us/111831", "s": "read", "said": "The page doesn't list battery capacity in either mAh or Wh."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://support.apple.com/en-us/111831", "host": "support.apple.com", "title": "support.apple.com/en-us/111831", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://www.apple.com/iphone-15/specs/", "host": "apple.com", "title": "specs page", "s": "read"}, {"u": "https://hothardware.com/news/battery-capacities-apple-iphone-15-models", "host": "hothardware.com", "title": "HotHardware", "s": "found"}, {"u": "https://www.mactech.com/2023/09/15/battery-capacity-of-the-iphone-15-line-up-revealed-in-chinese-regulatory-database/amp/", "host": "mactech.com", "title": "MacTech", "s": "found"}], "labels": ["official", "secondhand"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 2, "read": 2, "blocked": 0}}, "S-skip1": {"q": "Give me three names for a bakery.", "prompt": "Give me three names for a bakery.", "named": false, "effort": "medium", "skill": false, "html": "<ol class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Crumb &amp; Co.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The Rolling Pin</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Golden Hour Bakehouse</li></ol>", "trail": [], "srcs": [], "labels": [], "n": {"search": 0, "open": 0, "read": 0, "blocked": 0}}, "S-skip2": {"q": "What is the capital of France?", "prompt": "What is the capital of France?", "named": false, "effort": "medium", "skill": false, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The capital of France is Paris.</p>", "trail": [], "srcs": [], "labels": [], "n": {"search": 0, "open": 0, "read": 0, "blocked": 0}}, "S-skip3": {"q": "Is it better to rent or buy a home?", "prompt": "Is it better to rent or buy a home?", "named": false, "effort": "medium", "skill": false, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">It depends on your situation. Neither is better in general.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Buying tends to win when:</strong></p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">You'll stay put for roughly 5 to 7 years or more. Closing costs and selling costs (often 8 to 10% of the price combined) take years to recover.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">The monthly cost of owning (mortgage interest, property tax, insurance, maintenance) is close to the cost of renting a comparable place.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">You have a stable income and an emergency fund left over after the down payment.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">You want control over the space and a fixed housing payment. A fixed-rate mortgage doesn't rise the way rent does.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Renting tends to win when:</strong></p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">You might move within a few years, for a job, relationship, or just to see how you like the area.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Buying costs much more per month than renting. In many expensive metros, the price-to-rent ratio is high enough that renting and investing the difference comes out ahead.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">You'd have to drain your savings for the down payment.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">You don't want to pay for repairs and upkeep. A common rule of thumb is to budget 1 to 2% of the home's value per year.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Common mistakes:</strong></p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Comparing the mortgage payment to rent and ignoring taxes, insurance, maintenance, and closing costs.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Treating a home as a guaranteed investment. Prices can stagnate or fall locally, and your equity is hard to access.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Forgetting that the down payment has an opportunity cost. That money could be invested instead.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Quick test:</strong> Divide the home price by the annual rent for a similar place. Under about 15 usually favors buying, and over about 20 usually favors renting. This is only a rule of thumb. Current mortgage rates, local prices, and how long you'll stay can change the answer.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">If you tell me your city, the home price and rent you're comparing, your down payment, and how long you expect to stay, I can run a rough rent-versus-buy comparison. I can also look up current mortgage rates.</p>", "trail": [], "srcs": [], "labels": [], "n": {"search": 0, "open": 0, "read": 0, "blocked": 0}}, "N-purpose": {"q": "How many players does each team have on the field in a soccer match?", "prompt": "Use the horses-mouth skill. How many players does each team have on the field in a soccer match?", "named": true, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">Each team has up to <strong>11 players</strong> on the field, and one of them must be the goalkeeper. (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>)</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"official\" data-s=\"\">The IFAB Laws of the Game, Law 3.1, says: \"A match is played by two teams, each with a maximum of eleven players; one must be the goalkeeper.\" The same law says a match may not start or continue if either team has fewer than seven players. (<mark class=\"lb lb-official\">official</mark>; current edition, read 2026-10-10)</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/the-players/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IFAB, Law 3 – The Players</a></p>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "search", "q": "IFAB Laws of the Game Law 3 The Players number of players"}, {"t": "open", "host": "theifab.com", "path": "/laws/latest/the-players/", "url": "https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/the-players/", "s": "read", "said": "Law 3.1 sets the limits:\n\n- Maximum and goalkeeper: \"A match is played by two teams, each with a maximum of eleven players; one must be the goalkeeper.\"\n- Minimum: \"A match may not start or continue if either team has fewer than seven players.\"\n\nIn short, each side can have up to 11 players, one of them the keeper."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/the-players/", "host": "theifab.com", "title": "IFAB, Law 3 – The Players", "s": "read"}], "labels": ["official"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 1, "read": 1, "blocked": 0}}, "S-k04": {"q": "Which form do I use to renew a US passport by mail, and what is the fee?", "prompt": "Which form do I use to renew a US passport by mail, and what is the fee?", "named": false, "effort": "medium", "skill": true, "html": "<p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Use <strong>Form DS-82</strong>, the U.S. Passport Renewal Application for Eligible Individuals.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Fee (as of 2026-10-10)</strong></p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Passport book: <strong>$130</strong></li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Passport card: <strong>$30</strong></li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Book and card together: <strong>$160</strong></li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Optional expedited service: <strong>+$60</strong></li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Optional 1–3 day return delivery: $26.87</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">You pay only the application fee. There is no separate $35 acceptance (facility) fee on a mail renewal. Pay by check or money order made out to \"U.S. Department of State\". Don't send cash.</p><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Other points</strong></p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Mail the form yourself to the P.O. Box address on the form. Use a trackable service from the Post Office. UPS, FedEx and DHL can't deliver to a P.O. Box.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">If your name has changed, include a certified copy of the legal name-change document.</li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Renewal by mail or online is only for eligible adults. If you aren't eligible, use Form DS-11 in person instead.</li></ul><p class=\"f\" data-l=\"secondhand\" data-s=\"\"><strong>Source status:</strong> the State Department site (travel.state.gov) refused my direct fetches with an HTTP 403 error. So I did not read the official pages myself. The figures above are <strong><mark class=\"lb lb-secondhand\">secondhand</mark></strong>. They come from a search-engine summary of the State Department's pages. I couldn't check the delivery fee, because the summary said an older page lists a different figure. Please confirm the amounts before you send payment:</p><ul class=\"\"><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/how-apply/fees.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Passport Fees</a></li><li class=\"f\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/have-passport/renew.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Renew Your Passport by Mail</a></li></ul><p class=\"srcs-h\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"\">Sources:</p><ul class=\"srcs\"><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"0\"><a href=\"https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/how-apply/fees.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Passport Fees</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"1\"><a href=\"https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/have-passport/renew.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Renew Your Passport by Mail</a></li><li class=\"\" data-l=\"\" data-s=\"2\"><a href=\"https://travel.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/forms.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Passport Forms</a></li></ul>", "trail": [{"t": "skill"}, {"t": "open", "host": "travel.state.gov", "path": "/content/travel/en/passports/have-passport/renew.html", "url": "https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/have-passport/renew.html", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "open", "host": "travel.state.gov", "path": "/content/travel/en/passports/how-apply/fees.html", "url": "https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/how-apply/fees.html", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "search", "q": "travel.state.gov renew passport by mail DS-82 fee"}, {"t": "open", "host": "travel.state.gov", "path": "/content/travel/en/passports/have-passport/renew.online.html", "url": "https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/have-passport/renew.online.html", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}, {"t": "open", "host": "travel.state.gov", "path": "/en/passports/apply/help/forms.html", "url": "https://travel.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/forms.html", "s": "blocked", "said": "The server returned HTTP 403 Forbidden."}], "srcs": [{"u": "https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/how-apply/fees.html", "host": "travel.state.gov", "title": "Passport Fees", "s": "blocked"}, {"u": "https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/passports/have-passport/renew.html", "host": "travel.state.gov", "title": "Renew Your Passport by Mail", "s": "blocked"}, {"u": "https://travel.state.gov/en/passports/apply/help/forms.html", "host": "travel.state.gov", "title": "Passport Forms", "s": "blocked"}], "labels": ["secondhand"], "n": {"search": 1, "open": 4, "read": 0, "blocked": 4}}}, "kinds": [{"ask": "A product's price, specs, or release date", "reads": "The maker's own site", "run": "S-k01"}, {"ask": "A store's price or return policy", "reads": "That store", "run": "N-s3"}, {"ask": "A law, rule, or court ruling", "reads": "The official text", "run": "N-k03"}, {"ask": "A tax deadline, fee, or government form", "reads": "The agency that runs it", "run": "N-k04b"}, {"ask": "Health or safety advice", "reads": "The public health agency", "run": "N-k05b"}, {"ask": "A company's terms or plans", "reads": "The company's own pages", "run": "N-k06"}, {"ask": "How an app or code library works", "reads": "Its official docs, release notes, or code", "run": "N-k07"}, {"ask": "A statistic", "reads": "The group that measured it", "run": "N-k08"}, {"ask": "What a study found", "reads": "The study, not the news story about it", "run": "N-k09"}, {"ask": "Whether someone really said something", "reads": "The original video, transcript, or post", "run": "S-l-secondhand"}, {"ask": "Sports rules or results", "reads": "The league", "run": "N-k11"}, {"ask": "An event's date or details", "reads": "The organizer", "run": "N-k12b"}], "steps": [{"t": "Name the source before searching.", "run": "N-k06"}, {"t": "Read the source first, even when the page title looks unhelpful.", "run": "N-k03"}, {"t": "Search the source's own site if it did not show up in the results, or if its first page falls short.", "run": "S-k01"}, {"t": "Use other sites only for gaps, and say so.", "run": "N-s4"}, {"t": "Settle conflicts now instead of offering to check later.", "run": "S-hero"}, {"t": "No math on unconfirmed numbers.", "run": "N-s6"}, {"t": "Stop once the source answers.", "run": "N-k11"}], "labels": [{"k": "official", "def": "read on the official source, with a link", "run": "N-k04b"}, {"k": "calculated", "def": "math on official numbers, with the math shown", "run": "N-s6"}, {"k": "secondhand", "def": "found only on another site, which it names", "run": "S-l-secondhand"}, {"k": "unchecked", "def": "from memory, only when it cannot reach any source", "run": "S-l-unchecked"}], "desc": "Get facts straight from the official source. Use whenever an answer depends on a checkable fact that a specific person, company, agency, or project is responsible for, such as a price, date, spec, rule, law, policy, fee, deadline, requirement, statistic, quote, study result, version number, or how a product or piece of software works. Also use when the user asks to confirm, verify, or fact-check a claim, asks if something is true, or asks what the official answer is or what a source says, and whenever sources disagree or a claim rests on one secondhand source. Makes Claude read the official source before blogs, news, or forums, settle conflicts instead of offering to check later, and label each fact as official, calculated, or secondhand. Skip for casual chat, opinions, brainstorming, creative work, settled common knowledge, and routine coding where the answer is in the user's own files.", "skill": "---\nname: horses-mouth\ndescription: Get facts straight from the official source. Use whenever an answer depends on a checkable fact that a specific person, company, agency, or project is responsible for, such as a price, date, spec, rule, law, policy, fee, deadline, requirement, statistic, quote, study result, version number, or how a product or piece of software works. Also use when the user asks to confirm, verify, or fact-check a claim, asks if something is true, or asks what the official answer is or what a source says, and whenever sources disagree or a claim rests on one secondhand source. Makes Claude read the official source before blogs, news, or forums, settle conflicts instead of offering to check later, and label each fact as official, calculated, or secondhand. Skip for casual chat, opinions, brainstorming, creative work, settled common knowledge, and routine coding where the answer is in the user's own files.\n---\n\n# Horse's Mouth\n\nGet the fact from the official source, not from someone repeating it.\n\n## 1. Find the official source\n\nThe official source is whoever sets, measures, says, or first publishes the fact.\n\n| Fact | Official source |\n|---|---|\n| Product price, specs, or release date | The maker |\n| A store's price, stock, or return policy | That store |\n| Law, regulation, or court ruling | The official text from the government or court |\n| Government fee, form, deadline, or benefit | The agency that runs it |\n| Health or safety guidance | The public health agency or regulator |\n| A company's terms, plans, or policies | That company's own pages |\n| How software works, its limits, or versions | Official docs, changelog, release notes, or source code (in a project, the installed version) |\n| Statistic | The agency or group that measured it |\n| Study finding | The study itself, not news about it |\n| Quote or statement | The original video, transcript, or post |\n| Sports rules or results | The league or governing body |\n| Event dates or details | The organizer |\n\nEverything else is secondhand: news, blogs, forums, wikis, AI summaries, and search snippets. That holds even when they write \"X announced...\"\n\nIf no one owns the fact (history, broad science), use the most direct evidence you can find, such as original documents, official data, or peer-reviewed research. Say that is what you used.\n\n## 2. Procedure\n\n1. **Name the source first.** Before searching, decide who the official source is and where they publish.\n2. **Read the source first.** If it appears in results, open it before any secondhand page. Open it even if the title looks unhelpful. FAQs, help pages, press releases, PDFs, and changelogs often hold the answer. A search snippet is not a source. Open the page.\n3. **Search the source directly** if it did not show up in results or its first page falls short, for example `site:<domain> <topic>`. Try 2 or 3 of its pages before you fall back.\n4. **Use secondhand pages only for gaps.** Label those facts secondhand and name where they came from.\n5. **Resolve, don't offer.** If sources disagree or a fact rests on one secondhand source, check now. If the official source's own pages disagree, prefer the newest and most specific page, and mention the other. Never end with \"I can check X\" when checking X is one step away.\n6. **No unconfirmed math.** Combine numbers only when the source confirms every input. Label the result calculated and show the math.\n7. **Stop when done.** Once the official source answers everything, stop. Do not pad the answer with more pages.\n\n## 3. Answer format\n\nLabel each key fact or figure:\n- **official**: read on the official source. Link it.\n- **calculated**: math on official figures. Show the math.\n- **secondhand**: found only elsewhere. Name the source.\n- **unchecked**: from memory, only when you cannot reach any source. Say why.\n\nIn a long answer, one label per table row or section is enough. Name the place or version when the fact varies (country, plan, software version). Add \"as of <date>\" when it can change. If the official source says nothing, say so and list what you checked.\n\nKeep the reply short. Brevity applies to the answer, never to the research.\n\n## 4. Before you send\n\n- [ ] Did I open the official source itself? (If I have no web access, say so, label every fact unchecked, and name the source to check. If it would not load after 2 or 3 tries, say so and list what I tried.)\n- [ ] Is every key fact labeled?\n- [ ] Did I settle every conflict instead of offering to?\n- [ ] Is every calculated number built only on confirmed inputs?\n\nIf any box is unchecked, keep working. If a box cannot be checked (no web access, the source will not load, or sources still disagree after checking), say so in the answer and stop.\n\n## When not to use\n\nSkip it for opinions, brainstorming, creative work, and settled common knowledge, like a country's capital. Also skip it when the user wants secondhand views on purpose, such as reviews or commentary.\n"});
